## 4. Data model

Monetary amounts are integer paise. Reward rates are stored as rational numbers (`numerator` / `denominator`) or as basis points, never as bare floats in persisted rules. Display formatting happens at the API edge.

### 4.1 Identity and portfolio

```text
users
  id, email, phone, password_hash, display_name,
  created_at, deleted_at

sessions
  id, user_id, refresh_token_hash, expires_at

issuers
  id, name, country_code          -- e.g. HDFC Bank, IN

card_products
  id, issuer_id, name, slug, network, image_url,
  status                          -- draft | live | retired
  summary                         -- short reward blurb for browse UI

user_cards
  id, user_id, card_product_id, nickname, added_at
  unique (user_id, card_product_id)
```

A user stores the product they own. There is no column for PAN, CVV, expiry, or bank login.

### 4.2 Sources and versions

```text
source_documents
  id, card_product_id nullable, issuer_id,
  doc_type,           -- card_page | rewards_page | tnc | redemption | fees
  source_url, title

document_versions
  id, source_document_id,
  retrieved_at, content_hash, storage_key,
  effective_date nullable,
  http_status, raw_text

extraction_runs
  id, document_version_id, model_name, started_at, finished_at,
  status              -- succeeded | needs_review | failed
```

A card is `live` only when every reward rule attached to it has `verification_status = verified` and a `document_version_id`.

### 4.3 Reward rules

```text
reward_rules
  id, card_product_id, document_version_id,
  rule_type           -- base | accelerated | merchant | exclusion | cap | threshold
  category_code nullable
  merchant_key nullable
  channel             -- any | online | offline
  earn_kind           -- points_per_spend | percent_cashback | flat_cashback
  spend_unit_paise    -- e.g. 10000 for "per ₹100"
  points_per_unit
  percent_bps         -- 500 = 5.00%
  flat_paise
  priority            -- higher priority wins when two earn rules match
  confidence          -- 0.00–1.00 from extraction
  verification_status -- unverified | needs_review | verified | rejected
  effective_from, effective_to nullable
  last_verified_at

reward_caps
  id, reward_rule_id nullable, card_product_id,
  scope               -- rule | category | card
  period              -- statement | calendar_month | quarter | year | lifetime
  max_points nullable
  max_cashback_paise nullable
  max_counted_spend_paise nullable

redemption_values
  id, card_product_id,
  method              -- cashback | statement_credit | catalogue | transfer
  paise_per_point
  is_default
  verification_status, document_version_id

categories
  code, display_name  -- shopping, dining, travel, grocery, fuel, utilities, other

merchants
  id, display_name, default_category_code

merchant_aliases
  merchant_id, alias  -- "amazon", "amazon.in", "amazon pay"
```

Exclusions are `reward_rules` with `rule_type = exclusion`. A matching exclusion removes earn for that slice of the purchase. Caps are applied after earn, in the engine, using `reward_caps`.

MVP does not track how much of a cap the user has already consumed. The engine evaluates a single purchase and, when a cap exists, reports the cap and the benefit **for this transaction assuming the cap is still fully available**. Cap consumption across a month is Phase 2. The explanation must say that assumption in plain language.

### 4.4 Recommendations (audit)

```text
recommendations
  id, user_id, amount_paise, merchant_id nullable,
  category_code, channel,
  recommended_user_card_id,
  created_at, engine_version, latency_ms

recommendation_results
  id, recommendation_id, user_card_id,
  eligible, benefit_paise, points, cashback_paise,
  rate_label, cap_applied, exclusion_applied,
  rank, assumptions_json, rule_ids[]
```

This table is the source for Monthly Optimised Transactions and for accuracy reviews.

### 4.5 RAG chunks

Chunk text stays in PostgreSQL. The vector for that text is written to Chroma. A chunk belongs to one `document_version`. Replacing a version deletes its PostgreSQL rows and deletes the matching Chroma records (`where document_version_id`) before the new rows are inserted. Retrieval reads Chroma for nearest neighbours and PostgreSQL for the citation text and for keyword rank.

```text
document_chunks
  id
  document_version_id          -- FK, ON DELETE CASCADE
  chunk_index                  -- 0-based order within the version
  text                         -- the passage returned to the model
  token_count
  content_hash                 -- sha256 of text, used to skip re-embedding
  issuer_id
  card_product_ids uuid[]      -- empty when the page covers a whole issuer
  doc_type                     -- card_page | rewards_page | tnc | redemption | fees
  source_url
  section_title                -- nearest heading, may be empty
  page_start                   -- PDF page, null for HTML
  page_end
  prev_chunk_id                -- neighbour links for the expand step
  next_chunk_id
  tsv tsvector                 -- generated from text, for the sparse half of hybrid search
  embedded_at                  -- set when Chroma accepted the vector
  chroma_collection            -- collection name that holds this chunk's vector
```

PostgreSQL indexes: GIN on `tsv`, B-tree on `(issuer_id, doc_type)`, GIN on `card_product_ids`. There is no vector column and no pgvector index.

Chroma holds one record per card the chunk applies to, so a filter for one card cannot return another card's copy. Issuer-wide chunks get a single record with `issuer_wide = true`.

```text
Chroma record
  id                "{chunk_id}" or "{chunk_id}:{card_product_id}"
  document          same string as document_chunks.text
  embedding         float[768]
  metadata
    chunk_id, document_version_id, issuer_id
    card_product_id          -- empty string when issuer-wide
    issuer_wide              -- bool
    doc_type, source_url, section_title
    page_start, page_end     -- ints, omitted when null
    chunk_index
```

Collection settings: `hnsw:space = cosine`. Metadata values are scalars, which is why card ids are one record each rather than an array. The ranker never sorts cards by Chroma distance.

