## 5. Ingestion pipeline

Scheduled collection is a worker job. The recommendation request never fetches a bank site.

### 5.1 Source allowlist

Each issuer has `services/worker/sources/<issuer>.yaml`:

```yaml
issuer: hdfc-bank
user_agent: CardWiseBot/1.0 (+https://cardwise.example/bot)
min_interval_seconds: 15
entries:
  - url: https://www.example-bank.example/cards/regalia-gold
    doc_type: card_page
    card_slugs: [regalia-gold]
  - url: https://www.example-bank.example/cards/regalia-gold/terms.pdf
    doc_type: tnc
    card_slugs: [regalia-gold]
```

URLs in this file are the only URLs the crawler requests. Adding a page is a reviewed change to the YAML. The worker:

- Reads `robots.txt` for the host and skips a URL the file disallows. The skip is recorded on the job, not retried with a different path.
- Sends the configured user agent.
- Waits at least `min_interval_seconds` between requests to the same host.
- Does not log in, solve interstitials, rotate identities, or follow links that are not themselves on the allowlist. A PDF linked from an allowlisted HTML page is fetched only when that PDF URL is also an entry.

### 5.2 Schedule and jobs

| Job | When | Idempotency key |
|---|---|---|
| `crawl.source` | Nightly 02:00 IST, and on demand | `source_url + calendar date` |
| `process.version` | After a crawl stores a new hash | `document_version_id` |
| `extract.version` | After clean text is stored | `document_version_id + schema_version` |
| `chunk.version` | After clean text is stored, parallel with extract | `document_version_id + chunker_version` |
| `embed.version` | After chunks exist | `document_version_id + embedding_model` |
| `freshness.scan` | Daily 06:00 IST | date |

A job that is already `succeeded` for the same key is not run again. A failed job retries three times with backoff (1 min, 5 min, 15 min) and then sits in `failed` for an operator.

### 5.3 Fetch and change detection

For one allowlist entry:

1. `GET` the URL with a 30-second timeout. Record HTTP status, `etag`, `last-modified`, and byte length on the job row.
2. On status 304 or a body whose sha256 matches the latest `document_versions.content_hash` for that `source_document`, stop. No extraction, no re-embed.
3. On 200 with a new hash, write the bytes to object storage at `raw/{issuer}/{source_document_id}/{retrieved_at}-{hash}`, insert `document_versions` (`retrieved_at`, `content_hash`, `storage_key`, `http_status`), and enqueue `process.version`.
4. On 403, 429, or 5xx, record the status and retry under the job policy. Do not fall back to a mirror or a search-engine cache.
5. After three failures, raise an alert. The previous verified rules stay in force.

`content_hash` is the hash of the raw bytes. A second hash, `text_hash`, is computed after cleaning (section 6). If the raw hash changed but `text_hash` did not (tracking pixel, nonce in a script), the version is stored and marked `text_unchanged`. Chunk and extract jobs are skipped. This keeps layout noise from flooding the review queue.

### 5.4 What a new version is allowed to change

A new document version may create `reward_rules` in `unverified` or `needs_review`. It must not update a row whose `verification_status` is `verified`, and it must not set `verification_status = verified` itself.

When a reviewer later verifies a replacement rule, the worker sets `effective_to` on the previous verified rule for the same card, rule type, category, merchant, and channel, and sets `effective_from` on the new row. Until that moment users keep the last verified rule. If `last_verified_at` is older than 45 days, `freshness.scan` flags the card and the API adds the stale label. The rule still calculates.

