## 6. Document processing and chunking

`process.version` turns a snapshot into clean text, then `chunk.version` splits that text. Extraction and chunking both read the clean text. They do not depend on each other.

### 6.1 Clean HTML

1. Parse with a HTML parser. Drop `script`, `style`, `noscript`, `svg`, `nav`, `footer`, cookie banners, and nodes whose class or id matches a small denylist (`nav`, `breadcrumb`, `social`, `footer`).
2. Keep `main` or `article` when present. Otherwise keep `body`.
3. Turn headings into lines prefixed with markdown heading markers so the chunker can see section boundaries. Turn list items into lines starting with `- `.
4. Turn tables into markdown tables. Reward tables must survive as tables, not as loose cells.
5. Collapse whitespace. Keep paragraph breaks.
6. Drop runs of text that appear identically on more than half of the issuer's pages in the last 30 days (shared promo chrome). The first time an issuer is ingested this step is a no-op.
7. Store the result as `clean/{version_id}.txt` and set `document_versions.text_hash`.

### 6.2 Clean PDF

1. Extract text with a PDF text extractor, page by page. Record `page_number` on every block.
2. When a page returns almost no characters, run OCR on that page only and mark the blocks `ocr = true`. OCR text is allowed into chunks. Rules that cite only OCR text are forced to `needs_review` regardless of model confidence.
3. Merge hyphenation at line breaks (`re-\nward` → `reward`) when the join is inside a word.
4. Repeat the table and whitespace handling from HTML where the extractor exposes a table. Otherwise keep reading order.
5. Drop repeating headers and footers: a line that occurs on at least 60% of pages in the same vertical band is removed, except on the first page.

### 6.3 Identify cards and sections before the model runs

This step is deterministic and only prepares hints for extraction. It does not write `reward_rules`.

1. Load the catalogue names and aliases for the issuer (`card_products.name` plus any alias in source YAML).
2. Scan the clean text for those names. Record the character offsets. A page whose `card_slugs` is already set in the allowlist uses that list and still records offsets when the name is found.
3. Split the text into sections on heading lines. A section inherits the card names found inside it, else the names found in the nearest preceding section, else the allowlist slugs.

The extraction prompt receives these sections one at a time when the document is longer than 12,000 tokens. Shorter documents are sent whole, with the section list still attached so quotes can be tied to a heading.

### 6.4 Chunking

Chunker version is a constant in code, `chunker_version`. Changing size, overlap, or split rules bumps the version and re-chunks every current document version.

Parameters:

| Parameter | Value |
|---|---|
| Target size | 800 tokens |
| Overlap | 120 tokens |
| Minimum size | 80 tokens (shorter fragments merge into the neighbour) |
| Hard maximum | 1,200 tokens |
| Table maximum | 2,000 tokens, then split by row groups |
| Tokenizer | The Hugging Face tokenizer packaged with `BAAI/bge-base-en-v1.5` |

Split order, applied to each section from section 6.3:

1. If the section fits in the target size, it is one chunk.
2. Else split on blank lines (paragraphs).
3. Else split a paragraph on sentence boundaries.
4. Else split on the token window: 800 tokens with 120 tokens of overlap.

Rules that keep reward text intact:

- A markdown table is one chunk even when it is above the target size, up to the table maximum. The header row is part of that chunk.
- Above the table maximum, the table splits into groups of body rows. Every group chunk starts with the header row and the section title.
- A chunk does not cross a PDF page boundary unless the break would cut a table. When neighbouring pages are joined by overlap, `page_start` and `page_end` record both pages.
- Overlap is copied text, not a second logical section. `chunk_index` still increases by one. The overlap region lives in both chunks so a rate and its cap, often a sentence apart, land together in at least one chunk.
- Chunks under the minimum size merge forward. The last chunk merges backward.

Each chunk records `section_title`, `page_start`, `page_end`, `card_product_ids` from the section hint, `doc_type`, `source_url`, `issuer_id`, and neighbour ids. `text` is the exact string that will be embedded and later shown as a citation. No bytes are added that were not in the clean text, other than the repeated table header on a split table.

### 6.5 Embedding

Model: Hugging Face `BAAI/bge-base-en-v1.5`, loaded with `sentence-transformers` (`SentenceTransformer`). Output size is 768. The worker and the API load the same model id from `HUGGINGFACE_EMBEDDING_MODEL`. Weights come from the Hugging Face Hub on first use and then from the local cache. No embedding API key is required.

`sentence-transformers` L2-normalises this model's vectors. Chroma is configured for cosine, so distance is `1 - cosine_similarity`.

- Passages. The string embedded is `"{section_title}\n\n{text}"` when a title exists, otherwise `text`. No query instruction is added. `document_chunks.text` stays the citation body, without the title prefix.
- Queries. Prefix the template from section 7.2 with the BGE instruction `Represent this sentence for searching relevant passages: `. Passages and queries must use the same model. Mixing the prefix onto a passage, or omitting it on a query, is a bug.
- Encode passages in batches of 64. Skip a chunk whose `content_hash` already has `embedded_at` set for this collection.
- Upsert into the active Chroma collection. One record per linked card product, or one issuer-wide record, as in section 4.5. Then set `embedded_at` and `chroma_collection` on the PostgreSQL row.
- `embed.version` is `succeeded` only when every chunk for the version has `embedded_at` set and Chroma returns that chunk id.

A failed batch leaves `embedded_at` null. Those chunks are excluded from retrieval. Extraction can still produce draft rules from the clean text. Citations wait until the upsert succeeds.

### 6.6 Structured extraction

`extract.version` runs after clean text exists. For each section produced in section 6.3:

1. Build a prompt that contains the section text, the card-name hints, the closed set of `category_code` and `earn_kind` values, and the JSON schema for a rule. The instruction set says: copy numbers only from the section, every numeric field includes the source sentence in `source_quote`, use null when the section does not state the field, do not infer a rupee value for a point unless the section states one.
2. Call Groq (`GROQ_MODEL`, default `llama-3.3-70b-versatile`) with temperature 0 and a JSON response. The client uses `GROQ_API_KEY` against Groq's OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint.
3. Validate the payload in code:
   - `category_code` is in the enum or null.
   - `earn_kind` matches the fields that are filled (`percent_bps` set only for `percent_cashback`, and so on).
   - Rates sit inside bounds: percent at or under 100%, points per ₹100 at or under 100, paise per point at or under 10,000. Outside bounds → `needs_review`, row still stored.
   - `source_quote` is a substring of the section. A quote that cannot be found is dropped and the rule is `needs_review`. The character offsets of a found quote are stored so the UI can highlight them.
   - Conflicting earn rules for the same card, category, merchant, and channel in one version → all of those rows `needs_review`.
4. Set `confidence` from the validator, not from a model self-score: 0.9 when quote, bounds, and a single card match; 0.5 when the quote matched but the card hint was issuer-wide; 0.2 when the quote failed or OCR was the only source.
5. Persist `reward_rules` with `verification_status = needs_review` when confidence is below 0.85 or any flag above fired. Otherwise `unverified`. Both states are invisible to `POST /recommendations`.

Caps, exclusions, thresholds, and redemption rows use the same call and the same gates, with their own schema objects (`reward_caps`, exclusion rules, `redemption_values`).

### 6.7 Human verification

The internal review screen shows the draft rule, the quote highlighted inside the chunk, the source URL, and the diff against the current verified rule when one exists.

The reviewer chooses `verified` or `rejected` and may edit a field before verifying. An edit records `edited_by` and the previous model value. Verification stamps `last_verified_at` and `document_version_id`. Rejection keeps the previous verified rule active.

A card product flips to `live` when it has at least one verified base earn rule and one verified default redemption value (or a verified cashback rule that needs no point conversion). Until then it is `draft` and absent from search.

