## 10. Frontend

The product UI is the Expo app in `apps/mobile`. It is the only client for v1. Android and iOS share one codebase. Expo web is a development target so the same screens can be exercised in a browser at a centred 400-point column. There is no separate staff UI in the mobile app. Reviewers use the internal API.

The device renders figures, ranks, citations, and freshness labels that the API has already computed. A polished screen is a precise presentation of that response, with clear hierarchy, restrained motion, and type that makes the rupee amount the first thing a person reads.

### 10.1 Design intent

CardWise should feel like a well-set paper statement, not a marketplace and not a dashboard of charts. One decision occupies the screen. Decoration never competes with the recommended card and its rupee value.

Craft rules for every screen:

- One primary action. It sits in the thumb zone, full width, and uses the brand fill.
- The largest type on a screen is the number or the name the user came to see. On Result that is the benefit. On Recommend that is the amount they are about to type.
- Colour is mostly warm paper, ink, and a deep green. A brass accent appears only on the recommended mark.
- Surfaces are flat, with a hairline and a 1-point shadow. No glass, no gradients across whole screens, no illustration characters.
- Motion explains a change of state. It lasts under 400 ms. It never loops.
- Every interactive control has a pressed state, a disabled state, and a label that a screen reader can reach.
- Indian rupee formatting, with the rupee symbol set in the same face as the digits.

Reference frame is a 390 × 844 point phone. Layouts reflow from 360 to 428 wide. On web, the column is 400 points, paper background fills the viewport, and the column sits centred with the same 16-point padding.

