### 10.7 Motion

Durations and curves live in `src/motion.ts`.

| Name | Time | Curve | Use |
|---|---|---|---|
| `press` | 120 ms | ease-out | Button scale, chip colour |
| `swap` | 200 ms | ease-out | Segmented control, row expand height |
| `enter` | 320 ms | cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.8, 0.2, 1) | Result hero and amount |
| `settle` | 400 ms | ease-out | Display number arriving at its final value |

The result amount animates from 70% of the final rupee value to the final value over `settle`, using the number already in the response. It is a presentation of a known figure. Reduced motion, from `AccessibilityInfo`, skips translation and the number ramp. Opacity fades of 120 ms remain.

Screen transitions: auth and pushed screens use the platform horizontal slide. Result uses a fade plus an 8-point upward settle so the hero reads as a new surface. Tab changes are instant. No shared-element animation in v1.

Haptics, via `expo-haptics`:

- Selection tick when a chip changes.
- Success notification when a recommendation returns a winner.
- Warning notification when every card is ineligible.
- Light impact when a card is added to the portfolio.

Haptics are skipped when the system has them disabled.

### 10.8 Shared components

Components live in `src/components`. Each one takes content and state as props. None of them call the network.

**`Screen`.** `paper` background, safe area, and a `ScrollView` that dismisses the keyboard on drag. A sticky footer slot pins the primary button above the keyboard and the home indicator, with 16 points of padding and a top hairline only when the body is scrolled. The footer is outside the scroll so the action stays visible.

**`Button`.** Four roles. `primary` is `brand` fill, `brandInk` Outfit Medium 16. `secondary` is transparent with a `line` border and `ink` label. `ghost` is text-only in `brand`. `danger` is text-only in `danger` unless it is the sheet confirm, which is a full `danger` fill. While a mutation runs, the label swaps for a 20-point spinner and the control is disabled. Pressed state uses `press` from section 10.7.

**`TextField`.** Label in `Label` above the field, 8 points of gap. The field is 52 tall, `card` fill, 12 radius, 1-point `line`. Focus moves the border to `brand` at 1.5 points. Error replaces the border with `danger`, fills `dangerWash`, and shows `Caption` in `danger` 4 points under the field. Password fields have a Show/Hide control inside the trailing 44-point zone.

**`AmountField`.** The recommend screen's dominant control. Height 72. Fraunces 40, `ink`, tabular numbers, a `₹` prefix in Fraunces 32 `faint` until the user types, then `ink`. Numeric keyboard, decimal pad. The field strips characters other than digits and a single dot, and limits the fraction to two digits. Placeholder is `0` in `faint`. A blinking caret is the system caret. Under the field, a `Caption` in `faint` reads "Indian rupees".

**`Segmented`.** Two options, used for Email/Mobile and for Online/Offline. Track is `line` at 36 tall, radius 12, padding 3. The selected pill is `card` with the list-card shadow and an `ink` label. The unselected label is `body`. The pill moves with `swap`.

**`ChipRow`.** Horizontal wrap, gap 8. Unselected chip: `card`, `line` border, `body` label, height 40, horizontal padding 14. Selected chip: `brand` fill, `brandInk` label. Category chips use this. They wrap onto a second line on a 390-point width rather than scrolling, so every category is visible without a gesture.

**`IssuerPlate`.** 48 by 32, radius 6. Bank colour from section 10.4, initials in Outfit Medium 11 `brandInk`, centred. When `image_url` is present, the same frame shows the image with a `line` border and no plate colour.

**`CardRow`.** A `card` surface, radius 16, padding 14, shadow from section 10.6. Left: `IssuerPlate`. Centre: card name in Headline 18 (Outfit Medium 18 / 24), bank in Caption `faint`, one summary line in Body 14/20 truncated to two lines. Right: the action. Rows are separated by 12 points, not by dividers. Pressed state darkens the surface by mixing 4% `ink`.

**`BenefitRow`.** Padding 16. Name in Outfit Medium 16. Benefit in Outfit Medium 16 tabular, aligned trailing. Rate in Caption `body` on a second line. Ineligible rows keep the name and replace the benefit with "₹0", and put the reason ("Excluded", "Below minimum", "No matching reward") in Caption `body`. The winning row has a 2-point `brass` rule above the name and a Caption in `brass` reading "Recommended". Expanding the row uses `swap` on height and reveals points or cashback, assumptions for that card, `Freshness`, and a Source text button.

**`Freshness`.** A pill, height 24, horizontal padding 8, radius 999. Verified: `brandWash` background, `brand` Caption, "Verified 12 Mar 2026". Stale (API flag, last verified older than 45 days): `warnWash` background, `warn` Caption, "Verified 12 Jan 2026 · needs a check". The date format is `d MMM yyyy` in the device locale, with a fixed English month abbreviation so the pill width stays stable.

**`EmptyState`.** Vertically centred in the space above the tab bar. A 40-point brass rule, 32 points wide, centred. Title in Title (Fraunces 32). One sentence in Body `body`, max width 280, centred. One `primary` button, not full width, min width 200, centred, 24 points below the sentence.

**`Sheet`.** Modal bottom sheet for destructive confirms. Scrim is `ink` at 40%. The sheet uses the shape in section 10.6. Title in Headline, body in Body, then a row of two buttons: `secondary` Cancel, and the confirm. Dragging down or tapping the scrim dismisses when the action is not in flight.

**`Skeleton`.** `line` blocks with a 1.2-second opacity pulse between 0.5 and 1. Radius matches the content they stand in for. Reduced motion holds them at 0.7 opacity with no pulse.

**`ErrorBanner`.** Sits in the footer above the button, or under a list. `dangerWash` fill, radius 12, padding 12. `error.message` in Body `danger`. A `ghost` Retry appears for network failures and HTTP 5xx.

### 10.9 Screens

**Welcome** (`app/index.tsx`)

The screen is mostly paper, with the content set in the lower half so the wordmark has air.

- Top 18% of the screen is empty paper.
- A 32-by-2 `brass` rule.
- 16 points below it, the wordmark "CardWise" in Wordmark (Fraunces 40).
- 8 points below, Body `body`: "Use the card that pays you more."
- 48 points below, a footer stack: `primary` "Create account", then `secondary` "Continue with Gmail" (a G monogram and the label), then `secondary` "Sign in", then a `ghost` "Forgot password" centred.

No carousel, no bank logos, no reward percentages. The first screen is an invitation, not a catalogue.

**Register** (`app/(auth)/register.tsx`)

Header title "Create account". Content, top to bottom, gap 16:

- `Segmented`: Email, Mobile. Email is the initial selection.
- `TextField` display name, optional, hint Caption "Shown on your account".
- `TextField` email, keyboard `email-address`, or mobile with `phone-pad` and a fixed `+91` prefix in `body` inside the field. The prefix is not part of the submitted national number. The client sends E.164 `+91` plus the ten digits.
- `TextField` password, secure, hint "At least 8 characters".
- Footer `primary` "Create account".
- Above that button, `secondary` "Continue with Gmail".

`POST /v1/auth/register`. Email success stores tokens and replaces the stack with Recommend. Mobile success pushes OTP with the phone in params. Duplicate contact and a short password render in the field's error slot. The button stays disabled until the contact field matches its pattern and the password is at least 8 characters.

**OTP** (`app/(auth)/otp.tsx`)

Header "Enter the code". Body: "Sent to +91 •••• 1234" with all but the last four digits masked. Six separate 48-point boxes, gap 8, Fraunces 24, one digit each. Focus advances on input and retreats on delete. Paste of six digits fills all boxes. The keyboard is numeric and opens on mount. Footer `primary` "Verify" calls `POST /v1/auth/otp/verify`. Under it, a `ghost` "Resend code" is disabled for 30 seconds and shows the remaining seconds in the label ("Resend in 18s"). Resend calls `POST /v1/auth/otp/request`. A wrong code shakes the row 6 points, three cycles over 280 ms, sets the error "That code does not match", and clears the boxes. Success stores tokens and replaces the stack with Recommend.

**Login** (`app/(auth)/login.tsx`)

Header "Sign in". The same Email/Mobile `Segmented` as register. Password field. `secondary` "Continue with Gmail" sits above the footer. Footer `primary` "Sign in" → `POST /v1/auth/login`. Below the footer button, two `ghost` links stacked: "Use a one-time code" (mobile segment only, requests an OTP) and "Forgot password".

**Continue with Gmail**

The control is on Welcome, Register, and Login. It opens the API at `GET /v1/auth/google/start`. The app does not embed a Google client id. When `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` is set, the API sends the person to Google. The authorized redirect URI is `{PUBLIC_API_URL}/v1/auth/google/callback` (default `http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/auth/google/callback`). Google returns the ID token to that callback. The API checks it, then sends the person back to the app already signed in. On a local machine with no client id, the same button asks for the Gmail address and signs that account in.

The API checks the token with Google, checks `aud` against `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, and accepts only a verified `@gmail.com` or `@googlemail.com` address. A new address creates an account with `auth_provider = google` and the name from the Google profile. An address that already has a CardWise account receives tokens for that account. A Gmail-only account that tries the password form sees "This account uses Gmail. Continue with Gmail."

